Japanese folklore and mythology consists  of many legendary creatures and  supernatural beings we often refer to  them as yokai if there are supernatural  being or Carmy if there are type of  shinto god  today we'll be taking a look at a  creature that can often be regarded as  both a yokai and Carmy  the Tengu are quite popular figures in  Japanese folklore and their unique  appearance has made them quite easy to  identify for even those in the Western  world the origins of the Tengu like many  other things in Japanese and Asian  folklore is thought to have derived from  traditional Chinese literature the term  Tengu is believed to have been borrowed  from the Chinese Chango a demon canine  figure that was often thought to  resemble a comet or a shooting star  sometimes even being referred to as the  celestial dog the first of a mention of  the Tengu can be found in the 23rd  chapter of the nihon shoki written in  the year 720 the book itself is  considered to be the second oldest piece  of classical Japanese history the first  of a documented account of the Tengu was  by a Buddhist priest who claimed that it  appeared as a large shootin star in the  form of a heavenly dog  the Tengu did have a go quite a strange  and unexplained transformation as it was  rarely ever seen as a dog but rather a  large bird of prey almost raven or eagle  like and this has led many to believe  that the origins of the Tengu may have  been from the Hindu deity Garuda much  like the later depictions of the Tengu  Garuda was seen as human-like but with  the wings of a bird and a beak the  behavior of the two did differ gruder's  thought to have been much friendlier and  the Tengu are dangerous and aggressive  it's thought that when Buddhism first  arrived in Japan images of Garuda were  combined with an ancient shinto bird  demon creating the Tengu the Tengu are  quite commonly seen as large birds of  prey but over the years they're  transformed into bird-like men human  figures were the wings of birds in a  beak the beak itself slowly transformed  into an unnaturally long nose which  along with their red faces has become  their most recognizable feature a  feature that is often exaggerated an  artistic works almost resembling the  fictional character of Pinocchio the  change from a beak into an elongated No  thought to ally in associate the tengu  or solitary Co or commie the leader of  the Shinto gods on earth who was also  commonly seen over large and pointed  nose the tangley were known for their  skills in martial arts as well as their  great strength and power where the Hindu  god garuda was shown to be a friend to  buddhism but angular thought to be quite  the opposite often disrupted and playing  tricks on the buddhist priests and  samurai they considered to be arrogant  the Tang goos long noses are also  thought to represent a hatred for  arrogance and Prejudice and they  themselves are also often associated of  Buddhism it was quite common for the  priests who only cared about personal  gain to use the Buddhist teachings to  mislead other people they would then be  punished and turned into Yama bushy  Tengu this leads us to our next topic of  discussion the different types of Tengu  the Yamabushi Tengu appeared human and  it's thought that they were able to  transform into man woman or child their  preferred appearance was that of an  elderly hermit or monk like figure quite  often barefoot and with their customary  long nose the second type of Tengu is  the karasu-tengu large crow like figures  which are often more bird than man these  Tengu are not seen as tricksters but as  evil and menacing spirits they would  often kidnap children start fires and  punish anyone they see damage in the  forest at times they were known to  abduct people who were walking through  the forest these unlucky individuals  would eventually be released but they  would return in the state of dementia  known as Tengu kakushi meaning hidden by  a Tengu there have been numerous  Japanese folk tales over the years that  feature the Tengu the Tengu z' magical  cloak is a story where a boy wandering  through the forest finds a regular piece  of bamboo he placed the bamboo to his  eye and began to use it as a telescope  and nearby Tengu who had been watching  the boy was so overwhelmed by curiosity  that he jumped down from his tree and  told the boy he was willing to trade for  a magical cloak that turned the wearer  invisible having tricked the Tengu the  boy accepted the trade and continued on  his mischievous ways with his newfound  cloak  the old man's lump removed is a story  where we see the Tengu as trickster like  spirits one day an old man was wandering  through the mountains he came across a  group of Tengu dancing and drinking he  decided that he would join them and the  tangu were so impressed by his dancing  that they removed the lump from his face  assuming that he would return the next  day and asked for the lump back when one  of the old man's unsavory and unpleasant  neighbors had heard of his good fortune  he decided that he would also visit the  Tengu hoping that they would do the same  for him when he asked the Tengu they  refused givin him the first lump in  addition to his own why exactly the tank  who did this could be down to several  reasons the old man asked him for a  favor they clearly had not earned may  have angered the Tengu or maybe he's  dancin just wasn't as good as the first  man the final story that I like to cover  today is that of a gambler who meets a  Tengu the Tengu then asked the man what  he's most scared of the gambler suspect  that this may be a trick says that he is  terrified of gold when the man asks the  same question the Tengu replies  truthfully saying that he's scared for  nee plants the man then grabbed one of  the thorny plants when the Tang goes  back was turned and proceeded to mock  him waving the plant in his face in  retaliation the Tengu then summoned the  man's greatest fear rainin down gold  upon him but of course the gambler was  not scared  he had just tricked the Tengu and he was  now rich many of these folktales seem to  have similar themes behind them the  Tengu quite often attempt to trick and  outwit humans who in turn do the same  knowing that if they are successful a  great prize awaits them throughout the  years the Tengu have been through a  series of transformations and the  personality and behavior does vary  drastically from evil bird-like spirits  who were feared by most to pranks the  spirits who thrive upon targeting the  arrogant and the prideful or even  protectors of the forest it's no  surprise of so much flexibility and  variety that the Tengu have become a  staple of Japanese pop culture appearing  in countless games animes and mangas  storylines that feature any kind of  yokai will often feature a Tengu or two  to me personally the Tengu appear as a  jack-of-all-trades of Japanese creatures  and that does create an element of  surprise when reading stories about them  as you never really know what type of  Tengu you're about to see  I hope you guys enjoyed today's video if  you have any thoughts or you'd like to  share any stories about the Tengu then  please do so in the comments below as  always I've been your host with ology  and fiction explained  [Music]
 

